Here is an example Java program that implements a menu puzzle:
```
import java.util.*;
public class MenuPuzzle {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);
        String[] menuItems = {"pizza", "burger", "fries", "salad", "soup", "tomato", "sandwich", "noodles", "steak", "chicken"};
        Random rand = new Random();
        int index = rand.nextInt(menuItems.length);
        String menuItem = menuItems[index];
        String scrambled = scramble(menuItem);
        System.out.println("Unscramble the letters to reveal a menu item: " + scrambled);
        String guess = input.nextLine().toLowerCase();
        while (!guess.equals(menuItem)) {
            System.out.println("Sorry, that's not correct. Try again.");
            guess = input.nextLine().toLowerCase();
        }
        System.out.println("Congratulations, you unscrambled the word!");
    }

    public static String scramble(String word) {
        List<Character> letters = new ArrayList<Character>();
        for (char c : word.toCharArray()) {
            letters.add(c);
        }
        Collections.shuffle(letters);
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(word.length());
        for (char c : letters) {
            sb.append(c);
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }
}
```
The program starts by creating an array of menu items, then selects a random item from the array and scrambles its letters using the `scramble` method. The scrambled word is then displayed to the user, who is prompted to guess the unscrambled menu item. If the user's guess is incorrect, they are prompted to try again until they guess correctly. Once the user guesses correctly, the program congratulates them and exits.
The `scramble` method takes a word and shuffles its letters using the `Collections.shuffle` method. It returns the shuffled word as a string.

