Here is a sample code snippet written in Java that implements a linked list data structure:
```
public class Node {
  int data;
  Node next;
  public Node(int data) {
    this.data = data;
    this.next = null;
  }
}
public class LinkedList {
  Node head;
  public LinkedList() {
    this.head = null;
  }
  public void insert(int data) {
    Node newNode = new Node(data);
    if (this.head == null) {
      this.head = newNode;
    } else {
      Node current = this.head;
      while (current.next != null) {
        current = current.next;
      }
      current.next = newNode;
    }
  }
  public void delete(int data) {
    if (this.head == null) {
      return;
    }
    if (this.head.data == data) {
      this.head = this.head.next;
      return;
    }
    Node current = this.head;
    while (current.next != null) {
      if (current.next.data == data) {
        current.next = current.next.next;
        return;
      }
      current = current.next;
    }
  }
  public void printList() {
    Node current = this.head;
    while (current != null) {
      System.out.print(current.data + " ");
      current = current.next;
    }
    System.out.println();
  }
}
The runtime complexity of the basic operations of a linked list are as follows:
- Insertion: O(1) if inserting at the head of the list, O(n) if inserting at the end of the list.
- Deletion: O(1) if deleting the head of the list, O(n) if deleting from the end of the list or middle of the list.
- Search: O(n) in the worst case, since we may need to traverse the entire list.
