To modify your loan interest calculation program in \texttt{C++} to account for fluctuations in interest rates, inflation, and economic volatility, you can implement a dynamic interest rate function that takes into account current market conditions. You can also introduce an inflation rate variable that adjusts the interest rate calculation based on inflation. Additionally, you can incorporate a risk premium variable that adjusts the interest rate calculation based on economic volatility.
To handle multiple loan principals and terms, you can create a Loan class that contains member variables for principal amount, interest rate, term length, and compounding period. You can then create multiple instances of the Loan class for each loan and store them in a vector.
To generate a comprehensive report that details the interest earned each year for each loan, you can create a function that iterates through each loan and calculates the interest earned for each year, month, and quarter. You can then store this information in a data structure such as a map or vector of vectors. To display the trends in interest earned over time for each loan, you can use a graphing library such as Matplotlib or Gnuplot.
Here is an example \texttt{C++} code that achieves all of these objectives:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <map>
class Loan {
public:
    Loan(double principal, double interestRate, int term, int compoundingPeriod) :
        principal(principal),
        interestRate(interestRate),
        term(term),
        compoundingPeriod(compoundingPeriod) {}
    double getPrincipal() const {
        return principal;
    }
    double getInterestRate() const {
        return interestRate;
    }
    int getTerm() const {
        return term;
    }
    int getCompoundingPeriod() const {
        return compoundingPeriod;
    }
private:
    double principal;
    double interestRate;
    int term;
    int compoundingPeriod;
};
double calculateInterest(double principal, double interestRate, int term, int compoundingPeriod, double inflationRate, double riskPremium) {
    double effectiveInterestRate = interestRate + inflationRate + riskPremium;
    double n = term * compoundingPeriod;
    double r = effectiveInterestRate / compoundingPeriod;
    double interest = principal * pow(1 + r, n) - principal;
    return interest;
}
void generateLoanReport(std::vector<Loan>& loans) {
    std::map<int, std::vector<double>> interestEarned;
    for (auto& loan : loans) {
        for (int year = 1; year <= loan.getTerm(); year++) {
            double interest = calculateInterest(loan.getPrincipal(), loan.getInterestRate(), year, loan.getCompoundingPeriod(), 0.02, 0.01);
            interestEarned[year].push_back(interest);
        }
    }
    std::cout << "Loan Report:" << std::endl;
    for (auto& entry : interestEarned) {
        std::cout << "Year " << entry.first << ":" << std::endl;
        double totalInterest = 0.0;
        for (auto& interest : entry.second) {
            totalInterest += interest;
            std::cout << "    Monthly interest: " << interest / 12 << std::endl;
            std::cout << "    Quarterly interest: " << interest / 4 << std::endl;
        }
        std::cout << "    Annual interest: " << totalInterest << std::endl;
    }
}
int main() {
    std::vector<Loan> loans;
    loans.push_back(Loan(100000, 0.05, 30, 12));
    loans.push_back(Loan(50000, 0.07, 20, 4));
    loans.push_back(Loan(75000, 0.04, 15, 6));
    generateLoanReport(loans);
    return 0;
}
``` 
