One potential challenge in implementing virtual reality technology in healthcare is the cost of the equipment and software required. Another challenge is ensuring that the technology is accessible to all patients, including those with disabilities.
To address these challenges, a revolutionary solution could be to develop a low-cost, accessible virtual reality platform that can be used in healthcare settings. This platform could be developed using Swift and could be compatible with a wide range of devices, including smartphones and tablets.
Here is an example of Swift code that could be used to implement this solution:
```
import UIKit
import SceneKit
import ARKit
class VirtualRealityViewController: UIViewController, ARSCNViewDelegate {

    @IBOutlet var sceneView: ARSCNView!

    override func viewDidLoad() {
        super.viewDidLoad()

        sceneView.delegate = self
        sceneView.showsStatistics = true

        let scene = SCNScene()
        sceneView.scene = scene

        let sphere = SCNSphere(radius: 0.2)
        let material = SCNMaterial()
        material.diffuse.contents = UIColor.red
        sphere.materials = [material]

        let node = SCNNode()
        node.position = SCNVector3(x: 0, y: 0.1, z: -0.5)
        node.geometry = sphere

        sceneView.scene.rootNode.addChildNode(node)
    }

    override func viewWillAppear(_ animated: Bool) {
        super.viewWillAppear(animated)

        let configuration = ARWorldTrackingConfiguration()
        sceneView.session.run(configuration)
    }

    override func viewWillDisappear(_ animated: Bool) {
        super.viewWillDisappear(animated)

        sceneView.session.pause()
    }

    func renderer(_ renderer: SCNSceneRenderer, didAdd node: SCNNode, for anchor: ARAnchor) {
        guard let planeAnchor = anchor as? ARPlaneAnchor else { return }

        let plane = SCNPlane(width: CGFloat(planeAnchor.extent.x), height: CGFloat(planeAnchor.extent.z))
        let planeNode = SCNNode(geometry: plane)
        planeNode.position = SCNVector3(planeAnchor.center.x, 0, planeAnchor.center.z)
        planeNode.transform = SCNMatrix4MakeRotation(-Float.pi / 2, 1, 0, 0)

        node.addChildNode(planeNode)
    }
}
```
This code creates a simple virtual reality scene using SceneKit and ARKit. It displays a red sphere in front of the user and detects horizontal surfaces using ARKit. This code could be expanded upon to create more complex virtual reality experiences for healthcare applications.

