To accomplish this task in Java, you can use regular expressions to identify sentences in Present Continuous tense and then replace them with the corresponding Present Perfect tense using the String `replace()` method. Here is an example code:
```
import java.util.Arrays;
public class TenseCorrector {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] sentences = {"I am forgetting my umbrella.", "She is finishing her homework."};
        String[] correctedSentences = correctTense(sentences);
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(correctedSentences));
    }
    public static String[] correctTense(String[] sentences) {
        String[] correctedSentences = new String[sentences.length];
        for (int i = 0; i < sentences.length; i++) {
            String sentence = sentences[i];
            String correctedSentence = sentence.replaceAll("(?i)\\b(am|is|are)\\b\\s+\\b(\\w+ing)\\b", "has $2");
            correctedSentences[i] = correctedSentence;
        }
        return correctedSentences;
    }
}
```
In this code, the `correctTense()` method takes an array of sentences as input and returns an array of corrected sentences. It uses a regular expression to match sentences in Present Continuous tense and replace them with the corresponding Present Perfect tense using the `replaceAll()` method.
The regular expression `(?i)\b(am|is|are)\b\s+\b(\w+ing)\b` matches words "am", "is", or "are" followed by a space and a word ending in "ing". The `(?i)` at the beginning makes the match case-insensitive. The `\b` is a word boundary to ensure that we don't match words like "this" or "kissing". The `\s+` matches one or more whitespace characters between the helping verb and the main verb. The `(\w+ing)` matches the main verb in Present Continuous tense.
The replacement string "has $2" replaces the helping verb and the main verb with "has" and the main verb in Present Perfect tense. The `$2` refers to the second capturing group in the regular expression, which matches the main verb.
The `main()` method in this example code creates an array of sentences, calls the `correctTense()` method to correct the tense of each sentence, and prints the corrected sentences to the console.

