Sure, here's an example code in Java that extracts data from a deeply nested tag in an XML file:
```java
import java.io.File;
import javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilderFactory;
import javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilder;
import org.w3c.dom.Document;
import org.w3c.dom.NodeList;
import org.w3c.dom.Node;
public class XMLParser {
   public static void main(String[] args) {
      try {
         File inputFile = new File("input.xml");
         DocumentBuilderFactory dbFactory = DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance();
         DocumentBuilder dBuilder = dbFactory.newDocumentBuilder();
         Document doc = dBuilder.parse(inputFile);
         doc.getDocumentElement().normalize();
         NodeList nList = doc.getElementsByTagName("tag1");

         for (int temp = 0; temp < nList.getLength(); temp++) {
            Node nNode = nList.item(temp);

            if (nNode.getNodeType() == Node.ELEMENT_NODE) {
               NodeList nestedList = nNode.getChildNodes();

               for (int i = 0; i < nestedList.getLength(); i++) {
                  Node nestedNode = nestedList.item(i);

                  if (nestedNode.getNodeType() == Node.ELEMENT_NODE) {
                     if (nestedNode.getNodeName().equals("tag2")) {
                        String data = nestedNode.getTextContent();
                        System.out.println("Data: " + data);
                     }
                  }
               }
            }
         }
      } catch (Exception e) {
         e.printStackTrace();
      }
   }
}
```
In this example, we first create a `File` object to read the input XML file. We then create a `DocumentBuilder` object to parse the XML file and create a `Document` object. We normalize the document to remove any whitespace and other extraneous characters. We then get a list of elements with the tag name "tag1" using the `getElementsByTagName` method.
For each element in the list, we get a list of its child nodes using the `getChildNodes` method. We then iterate through each child node and check if it is an element node with the tag name "tag2". If it is, we extract the text content using the `getTextContent` method and print it to the console.
Note that this code assumes that there is only one "tag2" element nested within each "tag1" element. If there are multiple "tag2" elements, you will need to modify the code to handle this case.

